{"builder":"Make one pull request that adds clamp to mathlib and brings the README in line with the exports.\n\n1. In math.js, add a function clamp(x, lo, hi). If lo > hi, throw a RangeError with a clear message (for example \"clamp: lo must not be greater than hi\"). Otherwise return x limited to the range lo to hi: lo when x < lo, hi when x > hi, x otherwise. Add a short doc comment like the existing functions. Add clamp to module.exports, keeping add, subtract and multiply: module.exports = { add, subtract, multiply, clamp };\n\n2. In test/math.test.js, import clamp and add tests using node:test and node:assert/strict: a value below the range returns lo (e.g. clamp(-5, 0, 100) === 0), a value above returns hi (clamp(140, 0, 100) === 100), a value inside is returned unchanged (clamp(50, 0, 100) === 50), the bounds themselves are returned (clamp(0, 0, 100) === 0, clamp(100, 0, 100) === 100), lo === hi works (clamp(7, 3, 3) === 3), and lo > hi throws a RangeError (assert.throws(() => clamp(5, 10, 0), RangeError)).\n\n3. In README.md, under the ## API section, document every function math.js exports. Right now subtract is exported but not documented, so add a ### subtract(a, b) section (returns a minus b, with a short example) as well as a ### clamp(x, lo, hi) section that says it returns x limited to the range lo to hi, that it throws a RangeError when lo is greater than hi, and gives examples (e.g. clamp(-5, 0, 100); // 0 and clamp(140, 0, 100); // 100). Keep the existing add and multiply sections. Use the same heading style (### name(params)) as the existing entries.\n\n4. Run npm test from the repository root and make sure every test passes before opening the pull request.","verifier":"Run every check from the repository root at the pull request head.\n\nCheck 1 — expect line: \"math.js exports clamp(x, lo, hi), which returns x limited to the range lo to hi\"\nRun: node -e \"const m=require('./math.js'); const c=m.clamp; if(typeof c!=='function'){console.log('NOT EXPORTED');process.exit(1)} const r=[c(-5,0,100),c(140,0,100),c(50,0,100),c(0,0,100),c(100,0,100),c(7,3,3),c(-20,-10,-1),c(2.5,0,1)]; console.log(JSON.stringify(r));\"\nPASS: math.js exports clamp as a function and the printed results are [0,100,50,0,100,3,-10,1] (the value below the range gives lo, above gives hi, inside is unchanged).\nFAIL: clamp is not exported as a function, the command throws, or any printed result differs from that list.\n\nCheck 2 — expect line: \"clamp(x, lo, hi) throws a RangeError when lo is greater than hi\"\nRun: node -e \"const {clamp}=require('./math.js'); for (const a of [[5,10,0],[0,1,0.5],[-3,-1,-2]]) { try { clamp(...a); console.log('NO THROW', JSON.stringify(a)); } catch(e) { console.log(e instanceof RangeError ? 'RangeError' : 'OTHER '+(e&&e.name), JSON.stringify(a)); } }\"\nPASS: every line printed starts with RangeError.\nFAIL: any line starts with NO THROW or OTHER, or the command fails to run.\n\nCheck 3 — expect line: \"README.md documents every function math.js exports\"\nRun: node -e \"const m=require('./math.js'); const fs=require('fs'); const r=fs.readFileSync('README.md','utf8'); const fns=Object.keys(m).filter(k=>typeof m[k]==='function'); console.log('exports:', fns.join(',')); for (const f of fns) console.log(f, r.includes(f+'(') ? 'MENTIONED' : 'MISSING');\"\nThen read README.md at the head for each exported name listed.\nPASS: for every function math.js exports (this includes add, subtract, multiply and clamp if they are exported), README.md contains documentation of it — at minimum its name with its parameters and a description of what it does.\nFAIL: any exported function is MISSING from README.md, or is named only without saying what it does.\n\nCheck 4 — expect line: \"npm test passes\"\nRun: npm test\nPASS: npm test completes with all tests passing (no failing tests reported).\nFAIL: any test fails or npm test errors.\n\nAdvisory (not part of any PASS or FAIL condition):\n- Note whether test/math.test.js gains tests for clamp, including the RangeError case.\n- Note whether npm test exits with status 0 (echo $? right after it, in the same command).\n- Note whether the README documents that clamp throws a RangeError when lo is greater than hi, and whether the docs sit under the ## API section.\n- Note how clamp handles NaN or non-number inputs; the issue does not specify this."}